 | `avm2_e4x_parse_bounded` | **collectable E4X** (seedling-wasm-leak L1). E4X used to be immortal (every node a GC root, every XML/XMLList wrapper pinned), so a game re-parsing a level document per room swap (Seedling) kept them all: the leaking runtime OOMs in a 64 MB arena (and in 256); the fixed one sweeps ~8,070 nodes per collection at a flat 5.14 MB with 1,035 live objects. The live half is a liveness lock under `AVM2_GC_STRESS=1`: identity across ticks, `parent()` navigation from a leaf held alone (the rest of its tree must survive), a detached subtree, attribute lists, namespaces, a list whose target object only the list reaches, and a `setNotification` closure. Also guards the E4X scratch frees (serializer builder, in-scope namespace lists, parser stacks) |
+| `avm2_strbuf_stale_prefix` | **append-in-place string buffers keep stale prefixes intact** (s22 w2-strbuf; `avm2_string_concat` → `strbuf_append`, `avm2_value.c`). A long `s += x` writes `x` into `s`'s buffer and returns a new header sharing the bytes, so the OLD header `p` loses `utf8[len] == '\0'`. Every row reads such a stale prefix after a later append, each through a different consumer (length, `==`, indexOf/lastIndexOf, charAt/slice, Number/parseInt, split, RegExp match, a property key), plus two branches off one stale prefix (`x1`/`x2`: the second must not clobber the first) and a prefix left behind while the frontier keeps growing in place (`b`). A consumer that read `utf8` as a C string would print `Number(q)=75`, not `7`. **A behavior LOCK, not a repro**: byte-identical on the pre-buffer baseline. Hand-assembled ABC (`create_test_swf.py`, top-level script code, no mxmlc in the cloud); the numeric rows pad with spaces, not zeros, because `avm2_string_to_f64` caps its digit copy at 380 characters (a separate open defect). Expected output is derived from ECMA-262 semantics; Ruffle confirmation is a w2-strbuf LOCAL ORACLE REQUEST |
